1
00:00:03,600 --> 00:00:05,111
In eighteen fifty-nine,

2
00:00:05,151 --> 00:00:09,562
Bernhard Riemann wrote a short
paper about counting prime numbers.

3
00:00:10,252 --> 00:00:14,868
In it, he made a guess that is still
unproven. Today, we are going to hear it.

4
00:00:17,308 --> 00:00:23,447
Prime numbers are the whole numbers greater than
one whose only divisors are one and themselves.

5
00:00:24,137 --> 00:00:27,392
Count them, and you get a
staircase called pi of x,

6
00:00:27,432 --> 00:00:29,782
which climbs one step at every prime.

7
00:00:30,472 --> 00:00:35,750
The steps arrive irregularly. Yet from
far away, the staircase looks smooth,

8
00:00:35,790 --> 00:00:40,089
and Gauss guessed that it follows
x over the natural log of x.

9
00:00:40,779 --> 00:00:44,282
A sharper guess is an
integral. Near a number t,

10
00:00:44,322 --> 00:00:48,269
roughly one number in every
natural log of t is prime.

11
00:00:48,309 --> 00:00:53,805
So we accumulate that density, and
integrate one over the natural log of t,

12
00:00:53,845 --> 00:00:55,060
from two up to x.

13
00:00:55,750 --> 00:00:59,262
Below one million, there
are seventy-eight thousand,

14
00:00:59,302 --> 00:01:04,657
four hundred ninety-eight primes. The
first guess misses by about six thousand.

15
00:01:04,697 --> 00:01:08,277
The integral misses by only
about one hundred thirty.

16
00:01:08,967 --> 00:01:14,981
The Riemann Hypothesis is a precise statement
about how small that miss must always be.

17
00:01:17,421 --> 00:01:23,261
Riemann's tool is an infinite series, the
kind you study in Unit Ten of Calculus BC.

18
00:01:23,951 --> 00:01:29,825
The zeta function adds one over n to the
power s, for every positive whole number n.

19
00:01:30,515 --> 00:01:34,652
By the p-series test, it converges
when s is greater than one.

20
00:01:35,342 --> 00:01:39,928
At s equals two, the partial sums
settle on pi squared over six.

21
00:01:40,618 --> 00:01:45,325
At s equals one, it becomes the
harmonic series. It never settles.

22
00:01:45,365 --> 00:01:49,352
It grows like the natural log
of n, slowly, and forever.

23
00:01:50,042 --> 00:01:52,733
The integral test explains both cases,

24
00:01:52,773 --> 00:01:57,836
by comparing the sum with the improper
integral of one over x to the s.

25
00:02:00,276 --> 00:02:03,284
Euler found where the primes
hide inside this series.

26
00:02:03,974 --> 00:02:09,166
For each prime p, the geometric
series one, plus p to the minus s,

27
00:02:09,206 --> 00:02:12,100
plus p to the minus two s, and so on,

28
00:02:12,140 --> 00:02:15,826
adds up to one over one
minus p to the minus s.

29
00:02:16,516 --> 00:02:19,993
Multiply these series
together, over every prime.

30
00:02:20,033 --> 00:02:22,793
Each whole number appears exactly once,

31
00:02:22,833 --> 00:02:26,884
because every number factors
into primes in only one way.

32
00:02:27,574 --> 00:02:32,320
So a sum over all whole numbers
equals a product over all primes.

33
00:02:32,360 --> 00:02:36,444
This identity is the bridge
between zeta and the primes.

34
00:02:38,884 --> 00:02:43,350
Riemann then let s be a complex
number: sigma, plus i times t.

35
00:02:44,040 --> 00:02:48,608
From the Maclaurin series for
e to the x, sine, and cosine,

36
00:02:48,648 --> 00:02:53,997
you can derive Euler's formula. E
to the i theta equals cosine theta,

37
00:02:54,037 --> 00:02:55,403
plus i sine theta.

38
00:02:56,093 --> 00:03:00,757
So each term, n to the minus
s, is an arrow in polar form.

39
00:03:00,797 --> 00:03:05,379
Its length is n to the minus
sigma. Its angle is minus t,

40
00:03:05,419 --> 00:03:07,569
times the natural log of n.

41
00:03:08,259 --> 00:03:12,063
Adding the series means
placing the arrows tip to tail.

42
00:03:12,103 --> 00:03:17,236
When sigma is greater than one, the
path spirals in toward a single point:

43
00:03:17,276 --> 00:03:18,494
the value of zeta.

44
00:03:20,934 --> 00:03:23,901
When sigma is one or
less, the series diverges.

45
00:03:23,941 --> 00:03:26,717
But the function does
not have to end there.

46
00:03:27,407 --> 00:03:32,707
Recall the geometric series: one,
plus x, plus x squared, and so on.

47
00:03:32,747 --> 00:03:38,362
It converges only between minus one
and one. Yet the function it equals,

48
00:03:38,402 --> 00:03:43,467
one over one minus x, makes sense
everywhere except x equals one.

49
00:03:44,157 --> 00:03:50,039
Zeta extends in the same spirit, to
every complex number except s equals one.

50
00:03:50,079 --> 00:03:52,884
This is called analytic continuation.

51
00:03:53,574 --> 00:03:59,322
The extended function is zero at minus
two, minus four, minus six, and so on.

52
00:03:59,362 --> 00:04:01,427
These are the trivial zeros.

53
00:04:02,117 --> 00:04:05,475
Every other zero lies
inside the critical strip,

54
00:04:05,515 --> 00:04:08,023
where sigma is between zero and one.

55
00:04:08,713 --> 00:04:13,326
The Riemann Hypothesis states that
all of them lie on a single line:

56
00:04:13,366 --> 00:04:14,831
sigma equals one half.

57
00:04:15,521 --> 00:04:20,754
More than ten trillion zeros have been
checked, and every one lies on that line.

58
00:04:20,794 --> 00:04:21,940
No proof is known.

59
00:04:24,380 --> 00:04:29,317
Turn the critical line on its side, so that
its height, t, runs from left to right.

60
00:04:30,007 --> 00:04:34,639
Along this line, zeta can be
rotated into a real valued wave,

61
00:04:34,679 --> 00:04:40,690
called the Riemann Siegel Z function.
Its size always matches the size of zeta.

62
00:04:41,380 --> 00:04:45,740
Wherever Z crosses from positive
to negative, or back again,

63
00:04:45,780 --> 00:04:51,313
the Intermediate Value Theorem, from
Unit One, guarantees a zero in between.

64
00:04:52,003 --> 00:04:57,702
Computer checks of the hypothesis begin
exactly this way: count the sign changes,

65
00:04:57,742 --> 00:05:00,465
then confirm that no zeros are missing.

66
00:05:02,905 --> 00:05:04,853
Now, we give each zero a voice.

67
00:05:05,543 --> 00:05:09,210
Every zero becomes an
instrument, placed at its height,

68
00:05:09,250 --> 00:05:13,321
and tuned to ten times that
height, in hertz. The first zero,

69
00:05:13,361 --> 00:05:17,837
at fourteen point one three, sounds
at one hundred forty-one hertz.

70
00:05:18,527 --> 00:05:22,182
A wave travels from left to
right. When it reaches a zero,

71
00:05:22,222 --> 00:05:27,024
that instrument lights up, and every
instrument already lit strikes with it,

72
00:05:27,064 --> 00:05:28,871
together, at the same volume.

73
00:05:45,978 --> 00:05:50,909
Listen to the spacing. The zeros seem
to keep their distance from one another.

74
00:05:50,949 --> 00:05:52,566
Very close pairs are rare.

75
00:06:10,632 --> 00:06:14,709
Thirteen zeros below height
sixty. Thirteen instruments,

76
00:06:14,749 --> 00:06:17,649
in a rhythm set by the zeros themselves.

77
00:06:20,089 --> 00:06:22,917
Why should every instrument
share one volume?

78
00:06:22,957 --> 00:06:25,531
The answer is Riemann's explicit formula.

79
00:06:26,221 --> 00:06:31,591
It writes a weighted prime staircase,
called psi of x, as a smooth trend,

80
00:06:31,631 --> 00:06:33,814
minus one wave for every zero.

81
00:06:34,504 --> 00:06:36,430
By the rules of exponents,

82
00:06:36,470 --> 00:06:41,420
x to the power one half plus i
gamma becomes the square root of x,

83
00:06:41,460 --> 00:06:44,444
times e to the i gamma natural log of x.

84
00:06:45,134 --> 00:06:51,299
So each zero adds a cosine wave in the
natural log of x. Its frequency is gamma,

85
00:06:51,339 --> 00:06:56,729
the zero's height. Its size grows
like x to the real part of the zero.

86
00:06:57,419 --> 00:07:01,901
On the critical line, that real
part is one half, for every zero.

87
00:07:01,941 --> 00:07:04,893
Every wave swells at
exactly the same rate.

88
00:07:04,933 --> 00:07:08,719
That shared rate is the volume
we gave each instrument.

89
00:07:09,409 --> 00:07:12,869
If a single zero sat off
the line, at three quarters,

90
00:07:12,909 --> 00:07:18,152
its wave would grow like x to the three
quarters. It would drown out the others,

91
00:07:18,192 --> 00:07:22,180
and the primes would stray
further from their expected count.

92
00:07:22,870 --> 00:07:28,172
The hypothesis is a statement of balance.
No instrument is louder than the rest.

93
00:07:30,612 --> 00:07:32,737
Now, let the instruments play together.

94
00:07:33,427 --> 00:07:38,237
One wave alone is a gentle curve.
Add ten, a hundred, a thousand,

95
00:07:38,277 --> 00:07:41,221
and their sum sharpens into a staircase.

96
00:07:41,911 --> 00:07:46,646
At each prime, and at each power of
a prime, the waves line up in phase,

97
00:07:46,686 --> 00:07:48,238
and the staircase jumps.

98
00:07:48,928 --> 00:07:52,316
The primes are the moments when
the whole orchestra agrees.

99
00:08:13,611 --> 00:08:17,769
Series and convergence.
Euler's formula. Polar arrows.

100
00:08:17,809 --> 00:08:22,278
The Intermediate Value Theorem.
Logarithms, and integrals.

101
00:08:22,968 --> 00:08:29,943
The tools of Calculus BC carry us to the edge of
one of the great open problems in mathematics.

102
00:08:30,633 --> 00:08:34,252
What you heard is a translation
into sound, not a proof.

103
00:08:34,292 --> 00:08:37,585
Whether the balance holds
forever is still unknown.
